  {%- when 'how-to-choose-a-mattress' -%}
    <p>Choosing a mattress is one of the highest-stakes purchases in the home - and one of the least transparent. Marketing language piles up (“orthopaedic”, “cooling”, “hotel luxury”) while the decision that matters is simpler: <strong>does this bed support how you sleep, where you live, and how long you plan to keep it?</strong></p>
    <p>This guide walks through the factors sleep researchers and mattress engineers consistently point to - without medical claims or miracle numbers. Use it as a checklist before you reserve.</p>

    <h2>1. Start with how you sleep, not the brochure</h2>
    <p>Sleep position changes where your body needs pressure relief versus support:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Side sleepers</strong> usually need more cushioning at the shoulder and hip, with enough midsection support so the spine does not sag into a hammock shape.</li>
      <li><strong>Back sleepers</strong> typically do well with medium to medium-firm feel: the lumbar area should be held up without the hips sinking too far.</li>
      <li><strong>Stomach sleepers</strong> often prefer a firmer surface so the midsection does not drop and force the lower back into extension.</li>
      <li><strong>Combination sleepers</strong> benefit from a balanced hybrid feel that lets you turn without “stuck” sink.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you share a bed, prioritise the partner who is more sensitive to pressure points - and consider whether motion transfer matters for light sleepers.</p>

    <h2>2. Firmness is personal - but it is not random</h2>
    <p>Firmness is usually rated on a soft-medium-firm scale. Body weight and preferred sleep position shift what “medium” feels like. A useful rule of thumb from sleep-product research and retailer fitting practice:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Lighter bodies often experience the same mattress as firmer.</li>
      <li>Heavier bodies often need more deep support so soft foams do not bottom out.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not chase the firmest mattress because it sounds “better for your back”. Unsupported hardness can increase pressure points. The goal is <em>aligned comfort</em> - your preferred positions feel stable through the night.</p>

    <h2>3. Materials: what each layer is for</h2>
    <p>Most quality beds are systems, not single blocks:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Comfort layers</strong> (foam, latex, fibre) manage pressure and initial feel.</li>
      <li><strong>Transition layers</strong> stop you from feeling an abrupt drop onto the support core.</li>
      <li><strong>Support cores</strong> (pocket springs or high-density foam) carry weight and hold shape over years.</li>
      <li><strong>Covers</strong> affect breathability, hand-feel and temperature regulation at the skin.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Hybrid constructions (springs + foam) are popular because they combine contouring with airflow through the spring unit. Foam-only beds can feel plush and quiet; spring systems often feel more buoyant and easier to turn on.</p>

    <h2>4. Size: always check centimetres</h2>
    <p>Size names are not global. A “King” in the UAE is not the same width as a UK King. Always verify dimensions in centimetres against your bed frame and room.</p>
    <p>If you move markets or buy online cross-border, treat the cm figure as the source of truth - not the label.</p>

    <h2>5. Climate and cool sleep</h2>
    <p>Heat retention is one of the most common reasons people abandon a mattress. Breathable covers, open coil units and thoughtfully specified foams can help heat move away from the body. Be wary of precise “degrees cooler” claims unless backed by published lab methodology - look instead for construction that is designed for airflow and moisture management.</p>

    <h2>6. Longevity: replace the worn part, not the whole bed if you can</h2>
    <p>Comfort layers soften first. Support cores last longer when properly specified. A mattress designed so the comfort top can be refreshed can extend the useful life of the bed and reduce full replacement waste - useful if you want premium comfort without a full rip-and-replace cycle every few years.</p>

    <h2>7. How to evaluate during a trial</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sleep on it for consecutive nights in your normal positions - not a five-minute showroom lie-down alone.</li>
      <li>Note shoulder/hip pressure, lumbar support, heat build-up, and how easy it is to change position.</li>
      <li>Use a mattress protector; most trial policies require it.</li>
      <li>Keep notes for the first fortnight - adaptation is normal when feel changes.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="article-note"><strong>Editorial note:</strong> This article is general product guidance, not medical advice. If you have diagnosed spine or sleep conditions, speak with a qualified clinician about mattress needs.</div>

    <div class="article-faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>How long should a mattress last?</h3>
    <p>Many households replace every 7-10 years, but construction quality and body weight change that window. Softened comfort layers are often the reason a bed “feels done” before the core fails.</p>
    <h3>Is a firmer mattress better for back comfort?</h3>
    <p>Not automatically. Medium support that keeps the spine roughly level is usually more helpful than maximum hardness. Comfort is individual.</p>
    <h3>What should I decide first: size or firmness?</h3>
    <p>Confirm size in cm for your frame, then firmness and materials for your sleep position and climate.</p>
    </div>
  {%- when 'mattress-firmness-guide' -%}
    <p>Firmness is the first thing shoppers feel - and the easiest thing brands oversell. A “firm orthopaedic” label does not guarantee comfort. What matters is whether the surface pressure and deep support match your body and sleep style.</p>

    <h2>What firmness scales mean</h2>
    <p>Most retailers use a 1-10 scale (1 very soft, 10 very firm) or plain language: soft / medium-soft / medium / medium-firm / firm. Scales are not universal across brands, so treat them as relative within one product line.</p>

    <h2>Soft mattresses</h2>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> lighter side sleepers who need shoulder and hip cushioning; people who dislike “on top of the bed” feel.</p>
    <p><strong>Watch for:</strong> too much sink can make changing position harder and may leave the midsection unsupported for stomach or back sleepers.</p>

    <h2>Medium and medium-firm</h2>
    <p>This is the most common sweet spot for combination and back sleepers. You get contouring at the surface with a stable core underneath. If you are unsure, start here - then adjust softer or firmer based on a proper home trial.</p>

    <h2>Firm mattresses</h2>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> many stomach sleepers; some heavier back sleepers who bottom out on plush foams.</p>
    <p><strong>Watch for:</strong> pressure points at shoulders/hips for side sleepers; “hard” is not the same as “supportive”.</p>

    <h2>Body weight changes the equation</h2>
    <p>Two people on the same mattress can report different firmness. More weight compresses comfort layers deeper. If you and a partner differ significantly in weight, look for zoned support, a balanced hybrid, or a comfort system that can be tuned (for example, different comfort tops).</p>

    <h2>Support vs cushioning</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cushioning</strong> reduces peak pressure where bone meets mattress.</li>
      <li><strong>Support</strong> keeps your torso from sagging relative to hips and shoulders.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You need both. Soft all the way through can feel nice for five minutes and fail at 3am. Firm all the way through can feel “correct” in a shop and leave you shifting all night.</p>

    <h2>How to choose without a showroom</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Name your primary sleep position.</li>
      <li>Note body weight range (yours and partner’s).</li>
      <li>Prefer constructions that state layer purpose (comfort / transition / core).</li>
      <li>Use a trial long enough to adapt - often weeks, not a single night.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="article-note">If comfort needs change over time, a replaceable comfort top can be a more rational path than discarding an entire mattress when only the surface has softened.</div>

    <div class="article-faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Is medium-firm the safest choice?</h3>
    <p>It is a sensible default for many adults, especially combination sleepers - but side sleepers with sharp pressure points may still prefer a plush top over a supportive core.</p>
    <h3>Can a topper fix firmness?</h3>
    <p>A quality topper can soften feel; it rarely adds deep support. If the core has sagged, a topper is a temporary patch.</p>
    </div>
  {%- when 'cooler-sleep-and-mattress-materials' -%}
    <p>If you wake warm, sweaty or restless, the mattress is often part of the story - especially in hot, humid climates or modern sealed bedrooms. Cooler sleep is not one magic foam; it is a system: cover, comfort layers, core airflow, bedding and room conditions.</p>

    <h2>Why mattresses trap heat</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Viscoelastic foams</strong> can hug closely, reducing airflow at the body.</li>
      <li><strong>Thick impermeable covers</strong> slow moisture escape.</li>
      <li><strong>Heavy duvet + foam bed</strong> stacks insulation on insulation.</li>
      <li><strong>High ambient temperature</strong> leaves less thermal headroom overnight.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What helps a bed feel cooler</h2>
    <h3>Breathable covers</h3>
    <p>Open-knit or phase-aware textiles can move moisture and heat away from skin more effectively than sealed, plasticky knits. Hand-feel should stay dry rather than clammy after a few minutes of contact.</p>
    <h3>Air pathways in the core</h3>
    <p>Pocket-spring units create channels for air to move. Dense, unbroken foam slabs do less of this unless perforated or paired with ventilated designs.</p>
    <h3>Smarter comfort foams</h3>
    <p>Gel-infused or open-cell foams are commonly used to improve heat dispersion versus older closed-cell visco foams. Treat marketing claims carefully: look for construction details, not a single unverifiable temperature percentage.</p>

    <h2>Climate matters (Gulf and beyond)</h2>
    <p>In warm regions, daytime AC set-points and overnight humidity swings change how a mattress feels. A bed that sleeps fine in a cool UK bedroom may feel different in a Gulf apartment. Prioritise breathable construction if heat is already a complaint with your current bed.</p>

    <h2>Bedding habits that amplify mattress heat</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Waterproof protectors that are not breathable</li>
      <li>Memory-foam toppers on already warm beds</li>
      <li>Synthetic sheets with poor moisture wicking</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sometimes the highest-ROI change is the protector and sheets - then the mattress.</p>

    <h2>How to evaluate “cool sleep” when buying</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Ask what the cover is designed to do (moisture, airflow).</li>
      <li>Check whether the support unit is sprung or solid foam.</li>
      <li>Be sceptical of precise “°C cooler” claims without methodology.</li>
      <li>Trial in your real room conditions.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="article-note"><strong>Compliance note:</strong> We avoid unverified cooling percentages. Comfort and temperature perception are individual and depend on room climate, bedding and personal physiology.</div>

    <div class="article-faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Do cooling mattresses actually work?</h3>
    <p>Constructions designed for airflow and moisture management can feel cooler than sealed foam beds for many people - but results vary. Room temperature still dominates.</p>
    <h3>Is a hybrid better for hot sleepers?</h3>
    <p>Often yes, because coil units promote airflow compared with thick all-foam builds - assuming the comfort layers are not overly insulating.</p>
    </div>
  {%- when 'mattress-support-and-back-comfort' -%}
    <p>Search results are full of “best mattress for back pain” lists. Many overclaim. A mattress cannot diagnose or treat a clinical condition. What it <em>can</em> do is influence overnight pressure distribution and whether your spine is roughly supported in your usual sleep positions.</p>

    <h2>Support is not the same as hardness</h2>
    <p>Support means the mattress holds your body’s heavier zones (hips, torso) so they do not sink disproportionately. Hardness without contouring can leave the lumbar area bridging poorly and create new pressure points.</p>

    <h2>Alignment, simply explained</h2>
    <p>In side lying, a common goal is a relatively straight line from head to tailbone when viewed from behind. Too soft and the midsection may sag; too firm and the shoulder may not sink enough, pushing the neck and lumbar into awkward angles. Pillows matter here too - mattresses cannot fix an extreme pillow height mismatch.</p>

    <h2>Pressure relief</h2>
    <p>Shoulders and hips need enough give. If you wake with numbness or aching on one side, the comfort layer may be too firm for your frame - or the bed may be worn in those zones.</p>

    <h2>What to look for in a supportive mattress</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A clear support core (pocket springs or resilient high-density foam)</li>
      <li>Comfort layers deep enough for your sleep position</li>
      <li>Edge stability if you sit or sleep near the perimeter</li>
      <li>Honest trial terms so you can assess real nights, not showroom minutes</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When to get clinical advice</h2>
    <p>Persistent pain, numbness, trauma history or diagnosed spinal conditions deserve professional guidance. A mattress change can be part of a comfort plan; it is not a substitute for assessment.</p>

    <div class="article-note"><strong>Important:</strong> This is educational product content, not medical advice. We do not claim to treat, cure or prevent medical conditions.</div>

    <div class="article-faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Should I buy an “orthopaedic” mattress?</h3>
    <p>The word is used loosely in retail. Focus on construction, firmness match and trial - not the label alone.</p>
    <h3>Can a sagging mattress affect back comfort?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Visible hammocking or body impressions often correlate with worse overnight support. If only the comfort layer is tired, refreshing that layer (where the design allows) can be more sensible than replacing everything.</p>
    </div>
  {%- when 'when-to-replace-a-mattress' -%}
    <p>Households often replace mattresses on a calendar (“every eight years”) rather than on condition. A better approach: watch for performance failure, then decide whether you need a full replacement or a comfort refresh.</p>

    <h2>Clear signs the mattress is due</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sagging or body impressions</strong> that leave you rolling to a valley</li>
      <li><strong>Morning stiffness that improves when you sleep elsewhere</strong> (hotel, guest bed)</li>
      <li><strong>Noisy coils or broken foam</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Persistent odours or allergen build-up</strong> that cleaning does not resolve</li>
      <li><strong>Heat and moisture issues</strong> that arrived as foams collapsed and airflow worsened</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What wears first</h2>
    <p>Comfort layers take nightly compression. Support cores, especially well-made pocket springs, often outlast the plush top. That is why full replacement can be wasteful when only the surface has softened.</p>

    <h2>Refresh vs replace</h2>
    <p>If the design allows a replaceable comfort top, you can restore feel and hygiene at the layer that actually aged - while keeping a durable core. That aligns with a longer product life and less landfill, without pretending a tired foam top will recover on its own.</p>

    <h2>How to extend mattress life</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a breathable protector</li>
      <li>Rotate the mattress per manufacturer guidance (if applicable)</li>
      <li>Support it on a suitable base - slats with correct spacing or a solid platform as specified</li>
      <li>Address spills quickly</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Buying the next one with longevity in mind</h2>
    <p>Ask how the comfort system is meant to age. Prefer transparent layer specs over vague “10-year luxury” slogans. Pair longevity with a real trial and clear refund terms.</p>

    <div class="article-faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Is an 8-year replacement rule real?</h3>
    <p>It is a common average, not a law. Quality and use intensity vary widely.</p>
    <h3>Can I just add a topper forever?</h3>
    <p>Toppers can buy time. They do not rebuild a collapsed core. If you need deeper support, plan a proper refresh or replacement.</p>
    </div>
  {%- when 'hybrid-vs-foam-vs-innerspring' -%}
    <p>Most modern mattresses fall into three families. Understanding the structure helps you ignore buzzwords and buy the feel you actually want.</p>

    <h2>All-foam mattresses</h2>
    <p><strong>Feel:</strong> contouring, quiet, often “huggy”.</p>
    <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> motion isolation for couples; easy to move in some designs.</p>
    <p><strong>Trade-offs:</strong> can sleep warmer; edge support varies; deep support depends entirely on foam density and quality.</p>

    <h2>Traditional innerspring</h2>
    <p><strong>Feel:</strong> bouncier, more “on top”, classic bed response.</p>
    <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> airflow through the coil unit; familiar bounce.</p>
    <p><strong>Trade-offs:</strong> older continuous-coil designs transfer more motion; thin comfort pads can feel under-cushioned.</p>

    <h2>Hybrids (springs + foam/latex comfort)</h2>
    <p><strong>Feel:</strong> contouring surface with buoyant support underneath.</p>
    <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> balance of pressure relief and airflow; often strong edges with quality coils.</p>
    <p><strong>Trade-offs:</strong> typically heavier; price reflects more components.</p>

    <h2>Quick chooser</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hot sleeper / warm climate:</strong> lean hybrid or ventilated constructions.</li>
      <li><strong>Partner disturbance:</strong> foam or individually pocketed hybrids.</li>
      <li><strong>Easy turning / combination sleeper:</strong> hybrid or responsive latex blends.</li>
      <li><strong>Maximum hug:</strong> foam - but verify support core quality.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Spec transparency beats category labels</h2>
    <p>Two hybrids can feel opposite. Read layer depth, spring gauge/count where published, and foam densities when available. If a brand will not explain the stack, treat that as a signal.</p>

    <div class="article-faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Are hybrids always better?</h3>
    <p>Not always - they are often a strong all-rounder. Foam can be ideal when motion isolation is the priority and heat is managed with bedding and room climate.</p>
    <h3>Does coil count matter?</h3>
    <p>Higher counts can allow finer contouring, but coil design and comfort layers matter as much as the headline number.</p>
    </div>
